1
00:00:03,560 --> 00:00:05,400
I gotta tell you, the more I read about the

2
00:00:05,400 --> 00:00:08,580
primordial gravity wave discovery, the more excited I get.

3
00:00:08,590 --> 00:00:10,600
I know. Being an astrophysicist right now

4
00:00:10,600 --> 00:00:12,020
is like being a rock star.

5
00:00:12,020 --> 00:00:13,820
Only without the sex.

6
00:00:15,330 --> 00:00:17,400
Yeah, literally, none of it.

7
00:00:18,720 --> 00:00:20,420
What do you think about it, Sheldon?

8
00:00:20,420 --> 00:00:21,820
Meh.

9
00:00:22,790 --> 00:00:24,420
Are you kidding me? This may be

10
00:00:24,420 --> 00:00:27,280
the biggest scientific breakthrough of our lifetime.

11
00:00:27,280 --> 00:00:29,330
How can you, as a theoretical physicist,

12
00:00:29,330 --> 00:00:30,830
not care about this?

13
00:00:30,830 --> 00:00:33,020
Maybe it's because I'm not an elitist.

14
00:00:33,020 --> 00:00:34,390
What I'd like to know is:

15
00:00:34,390 --> 00:00:36,320
How does this gravity wave breakthrough

16
00:00:36,320 --> 00:00:38,170
help the man on the street? You know?

17
00:00:38,170 --> 00:00:40,040
Wh-Who's looking out for Joe Six-Pack

18
00:00:40,040 --> 00:00:42,520
and all his quantum physics concerns?

19
00:00:43,380 --> 00:00:45,620
Oh, my God, you're jealous.

20
00:00:45,620 --> 00:00:48,290
- Why would I be jealous? - Oh, I don't know.

21
00:00:48,290 --> 00:00:51,170
Maybe because the origin of the universe just got proven,

22
00:00:51,170 --> 00:00:52,700
the Higgs field just got proven

23
00:00:52,710 --> 00:00:54,250
and you've been working on string theory

24
00:00:54,250 --> 00:00:56,620
for the last 20 years and you're no closer to proving it

25
00:00:56,620 --> 00:00:58,330
than when you started.

26
00:00:59,210 --> 00:01:01,420
Yeah, well, I've had a lot on my plate.

27
00:01:02,290 --> 00:01:04,990
We happen to live in a golden age of television.

28
00:01:06,240 --> 00:01:07,830
Excuse me, fellas.

29
00:01:08,350 --> 00:01:10,080
Sorry for eavesdropping,

30
00:01:10,080 --> 00:01:12,380
but there actually was some big string theory news today

31
00:01:12,380 --> 00:01:14,880
- out of the Hadron Collider. - Really?

32
00:01:14,880 --> 00:01:16,980
Did they find evidence to support extra dimensions

33
00:01:16,980 --> 00:01:18,520
- or supersymmetry? - No,

34
00:01:18,520 --> 00:01:21,910
but they did find evidence that you'll believe anything.

35
00:01:23,980 --> 00:01:25,650
Why would you do that?

36
00:01:25,650 --> 00:01:27,570
You're a string theorist as well.

37
00:01:27,570 --> 00:01:30,790
Incorrect. I am a string pragmatist.

38
00:01:30,790 --> 00:01:33,080
I say I'm gonna prove something that cannot be proved,

39
00:01:33,080 --> 00:01:34,250
I apply for grant money,

40
00:01:34,250 --> 00:01:36,800
and then I spend it on liquor and broads.

41
00:01:37,780 --> 00:01:38,890
Later.

42
00:01:41,560 --> 00:01:43,330
Do you think he's right?

43
00:01:43,980 --> 00:01:48,110
Am I wasting my life on a theory that can never be proven?

44
00:01:50,910 --> 00:01:54,240
Maybe. But how great is Game of Thrones?

45
00:02:21,770 --> 00:02:23,130
Hey, you're up early.

46
00:02:23,990 --> 00:02:25,020
I couldn't sleep.

47
00:02:25,020 --> 00:02:28,680
I told you those Walking Dead pillowcases were a bad idea.

48
00:02:29,950 --> 00:02:31,760
No, that's not it.

49
00:02:31,760 --> 00:02:33,440
Is something bothering you?

50
00:02:33,440 --> 00:02:36,240
Yes, but you wouldn't understand.

51
00:02:36,240 --> 00:02:38,590
Oh, come on, try me.

52
00:02:39,440 --> 00:02:40,310
All right.

53
00:02:43,490 --> 00:02:46,440
I've devoted the prime of my life to string theory

54
00:02:46,440 --> 00:02:49,960
and its quest for the compactification of extra dimensions.

55
00:02:49,990 --> 00:02:53,310
I've got nothing to show for it, and I feel like a fool.

56
00:02:54,590 --> 00:02:56,100
Okay. I get it.

57
00:02:56,460 --> 00:02:58,920
I mean, not all the jibberjabber in the middle, but...

58
00:03:00,590 --> 00:03:01,340
I know what it's like

59
00:03:01,340 --> 00:03:02,700
to put your heart and soul into something

60
00:03:02,700 --> 00:03:04,170
and get nothing out of it.

61
00:03:04,670 --> 00:03:06,470
You mean your acting career?

62
00:03:08,530 --> 00:03:09,340
No.

63
00:03:09,550 --> 00:03:11,330
Your relationship with Leonard?

64
00:03:12,990 --> 00:03:13,980
No.

65
00:03:13,980 --> 00:03:15,290
Your failed attempt to go back to college?

66
00:03:15,290 --> 00:03:16,610
No!

67
00:03:17,620 --> 00:03:20,240
I'm saying you and string theory sound like a relationship,

68
00:03:20,240 --> 00:03:21,530
and I know what it's like to be in one

69
00:03:21,530 --> 00:03:24,340
and realize it's never gonna turn out the way you want.

70
00:03:24,960 --> 00:03:27,110
I said Leonard; you said no.

71
00:03:28,220 --> 00:03:30,800
I'm talking about other guys.

72
00:03:30,800 --> 00:03:32,120
Okay.

73
00:03:32,120 --> 00:03:34,810
Well, what do you do?

74
00:03:34,810 --> 00:03:37,120
You have to have the courage to end the relationship.

75
00:03:37,120 --> 00:03:40,450
You know? Break it off, shake hands, walk away.

76
00:03:41,790 --> 00:03:43,770
I don't know if I can do that.

77
00:03:43,770 --> 00:03:44,850
I know it's hard, honey,

78
00:03:44,850 --> 00:03:47,430
but in the end, that's how you grow.

79
00:03:51,620 --> 00:03:54,000
Penny, have you seen my good inhaler?

80
00:03:56,470 --> 00:03:59,170
Break it off, shake hands, walk away.

81
00:04:04,260 --> 00:04:06,850
Hey, how's dating two women going?

82
00:04:06,850 --> 00:04:09,150
Um, kind of hit a bump.

83
00:04:09,150 --> 00:04:10,880
When I was honest and told Emily

84
00:04:10,880 --> 00:04:13,380
she wasn't the only person I was seeing, it went great.

85
00:04:13,380 --> 00:04:15,380
So I tried the same thing with Lucy.

86
00:04:15,520 --> 00:04:16,750
And?

87
00:04:16,750 --> 00:04:19,000
She had mixed feelings.

88
00:04:19,990 --> 00:04:21,870
But when I said, "Emily was cool with it.

89
00:04:21,870 --> 00:04:24,440
Emily's the best. Why can't you be more like Emily?"...

90
00:04:26,520 --> 00:04:29,290
...those feelings became less mixed.

91
00:04:31,680 --> 00:04:34,810
Women. Who knows what'll set 'em off?

92
00:04:35,640 --> 00:04:38,470
At least now I can focus all my energies on just one girl.

93
00:04:38,470 --> 00:04:40,500
I hope I get to meet her as soon as possible.

94
00:04:40,500 --> 00:04:42,370
Why the rush? She isn't going anywhere.

95
00:04:42,370 --> 00:04:44,840
She is. But I like that attitude.

96
00:04:46,260 --> 00:04:50,690
Gentlemen, is anybody interested in my old string theory books?

97
00:04:50,690 --> 00:04:52,110
You're really going to do this?

98
00:04:52,110 --> 00:04:54,910
I am. Yeah, Penny pointed out that

99
00:04:54,910 --> 00:04:57,150
what I'm going through is essentially a breakup.

100
00:04:57,150 --> 00:05:00,170
And according to Cosmopolitan magazine,

101
00:05:00,170 --> 00:05:02,530
one of the most effective techniques for moving on

102
00:05:02,530 --> 00:05:05,420
is to get rid of all reminders of the relationship.

103
00:05:05,420 --> 00:05:07,460
- You're reading Cosmo? - Yes.

104
00:05:07,460 --> 00:05:09,830
As it turns out, there's an article

105
00:05:09,830 --> 00:05:13,380
on how to get over a breakup in literally every issue.

106
00:05:14,490 --> 00:05:15,090
Anyway,

107
00:05:15,090 --> 00:05:17,630
it suggests that I set these on fire,

108
00:05:17,720 --> 00:05:19,500
but the smell of burning books

109
00:05:19,500 --> 00:05:23,030
reminds me of church picnics in East Texas.

110
00:05:24,690 --> 00:05:26,100
What's this?

111
00:05:26,140 --> 00:05:29,590
That's just a doodle of a hyperelliptic Riemann surface.

112
00:05:29,590 --> 00:05:32,830
Oh, yeah. Wasn't that the basis of your postdoc fellowship?

113
00:05:32,840 --> 00:05:34,110
It was.

114
00:05:34,480 --> 00:05:36,910
This seemed so elegant at the time,

115
00:05:37,080 --> 00:05:40,660
but... now I realize I was just a simple country boy

116
00:05:40,670 --> 00:05:42,910
seduced by a big city theory

117
00:05:42,910 --> 00:05:45,720
with variables in all the right places.

118
00:05:47,340 --> 00:05:49,570
- It's going to be okay. - I know.

119
00:05:49,570 --> 00:05:51,700
As hard as this is, I have to move on.

120
00:05:51,890 --> 00:05:54,390
I can't keep postulating multidimensional entities

121
00:05:54,390 --> 00:05:56,080
and get nothing in return.

122
00:05:56,800 --> 00:05:58,440
I have needs, too.

123
00:06:04,330 --> 00:06:06,170
So, you and Emily still together?

124
00:06:13,310 --> 00:06:15,450
I spoke to Bernadette. She's free tomorrow night.

125
00:06:15,450 --> 00:06:16,500
Oh, okay, great.

126
00:06:17,360 --> 00:06:19,350
But if we're really gonna do a double date,

127
00:06:19,350 --> 00:06:21,620
then we have to go over some ground rules about Emily.

128
00:06:21,620 --> 00:06:23,770
Like when it turns out she's made of rubber,

129
00:06:23,770 --> 00:06:25,220
I don't say anything?

130
00:06:27,570 --> 00:06:29,040
She's very real.

131
00:06:29,040 --> 00:06:31,400
Oh, that's what it says on the box.

132
00:06:32,970 --> 00:06:35,410
Right next to "Dishwasher safe."

133
00:06:36,630 --> 00:06:37,490
See?

134
00:06:37,530 --> 00:06:38,670
This is exactly the kind of thing

135
00:06:38,670 --> 00:06:40,300
I'm worried about you saying in front of her.

136
00:06:40,300 --> 00:06:43,510
- I promise I'll be on my best behavior. - You better be.

137
00:06:43,510 --> 00:06:46,070
No jokes about how close I am with my dog.

138
00:06:46,980 --> 00:06:49,490
Or the truth about how close I am with my dog.

139
00:06:50,950 --> 00:06:51,700
You got it.

140
00:06:51,700 --> 00:06:53,650
No jokes about the year I took ballet.

141
00:06:53,650 --> 00:06:55,570
You took ballet?

142
00:06:56,340 --> 00:06:58,440
God, you never listen.

143
00:07:01,330 --> 00:07:03,260
Are you sure you want to do this?

144
00:07:03,260 --> 00:07:05,820
The magazine articles suggest that one of the ways

145
00:07:05,820 --> 00:07:08,240
to get over a breakup is a new look.

146
00:07:08,530 --> 00:07:09,880
What about your old look,

147
00:07:09,880 --> 00:07:12,500
well-groomed ventriloquist doll?

148
00:07:14,470 --> 00:07:17,500
- "Oh, my God, I do look like that." - Stop it!

149
00:07:19,580 --> 00:07:21,580
So, how do you want me to cut it?

150
00:07:21,580 --> 00:07:26,310
How about Bill Gates meets Nikola Tesla?

151
00:07:27,390 --> 00:07:30,170
So, business in the front, science in the back.

152
00:07:31,330 --> 00:07:32,390
Or I don't cut it

153
00:07:32,390 --> 00:07:33,890
and maybe just style it a little.

154
00:07:33,890 --> 00:07:35,570
Oh, I'm in your hands.

155
00:07:35,690 --> 00:07:38,870
Do a good job and I'll tell you Cosmo's ten dynamite tips

156
00:07:38,870 --> 00:07:40,870
to enjoy your PMS.

157
00:07:42,420 --> 00:07:43,280
Can you keep him quiet?

158
00:07:43,280 --> 00:07:45,170
Maybe jangle some keys in front of him?

159
00:07:45,170 --> 00:07:46,240
That doesn't work anymore.

160
00:07:46,240 --> 00:07:48,730
He just thinks I'm taking him to the doctor.

161
00:07:50,450 --> 00:07:52,960
So, now that you're no longer invested in string theory,

162
00:07:52,960 --> 00:07:54,620
what are you gonna pursue?

163
00:07:54,620 --> 00:07:56,970
Oh, there are so many exciting areas:

164
00:07:56,970 --> 00:07:59,330
black holes, dark matter...

165
00:07:59,330 --> 00:08:00,970
Oh, Leonard was telling me about dark matter,

166
00:08:00,970 --> 00:08:02,740
but I didn't really understand it.

167
00:08:02,740 --> 00:08:05,440
Don't feel bad. Neither does he.

168
00:08:09,350 --> 00:08:11,120
Okay...

169
00:08:11,810 --> 00:08:13,270
what do you think?

170
00:08:15,620 --> 00:08:18,710
Have you ever even seen a picture of Tesla?

171
00:08:19,350 --> 00:08:20,880
It actually looks good, Sheldon.

172
00:08:20,880 --> 00:08:22,180
- Doesn't it? - Yeah.

173
00:08:23,010 --> 00:08:27,040
I suppose I could see myself in a scientific boy band.

174
00:08:28,210 --> 00:08:30,880
Of course, I'd be the dreamy one and the smart one.

175
00:08:32,640 --> 00:08:34,010
Sorry I'm late.

176
00:08:34,770 --> 00:08:36,420
What did you do?

177
00:08:36,420 --> 00:08:38,880
I gave him a new look. It's cute, huh?

178
00:08:38,940 --> 00:08:41,660
Yeah, it's cute. That's the problem.

179
00:08:42,040 --> 00:08:43,650
I don't need other girls to see him walking around

180
00:08:43,650 --> 00:08:45,490
like sex on a stick.

181
00:08:48,230 --> 00:08:50,100
She's right. I'm too hot.

182
00:08:55,380 --> 00:08:57,020
I'm excited to meet Emily.

183
00:08:57,230 --> 00:08:59,710
Me, too. I just hope he doesn't blow it.

184
00:09:00,090 --> 00:09:01,590
Why would you say that?

185
00:09:01,720 --> 00:09:04,320
Because he's Raj-- that's his thing.

186
00:09:05,560 --> 00:09:07,320
Beckham can bend it, Ralph can wreck it,

187
00:09:07,320 --> 00:09:09,130
Raj can blow it.

188
00:09:10,190 --> 00:09:11,750
Look, I don't want it to happen,

189
00:09:11,750 --> 00:09:14,520
but odds are he's gonna find a way to...

190
00:09:15,890 --> 00:09:17,780
Oh, crap, I know that girl.

191
00:09:17,840 --> 00:09:18,580
How?

192
00:09:18,580 --> 00:09:20,660
In a bad way, very bad.

193
00:09:21,680 --> 00:09:22,300
Hey, guys.

194
00:09:22,310 --> 00:09:23,740
Whatever you hear tonight, just remember I love you.

195
00:09:23,740 --> 00:09:24,750
Hey!

196
00:09:30,140 --> 00:09:31,620
Emily, these are my good friends,

197
00:09:31,620 --> 00:09:33,090
Howard and Bernadette.

198
00:09:33,090 --> 00:09:35,240
- Guys, this is Emily. - Nice to meet you.

199
00:09:35,240 --> 00:09:36,350
Hi.

200
00:09:36,740 --> 00:09:38,190
Have we met before?

201
00:09:39,440 --> 00:09:41,500
Uh... no.

202
00:09:42,520 --> 00:09:45,240
I-I don't... I don't... think so.

203
00:09:45,240 --> 00:09:46,830
You sure? You look familiar.

204
00:09:46,930 --> 00:09:48,960
Well, you sure don't.

205
00:09:48,960 --> 00:09:51,460
You I know, you I know, you?

206
00:09:51,750 --> 00:09:53,760
Total stranger.

207
00:09:54,250 --> 00:09:57,320
Even if you had yummy candy, I would not get in your van.

208
00:09:58,690 --> 00:10:01,190
Did you go to that spin class on Green Street?

209
00:10:01,270 --> 00:10:03,090
That must be it.

210
00:10:03,100 --> 00:10:05,310
Since when do you go to spin class?

211
00:10:05,530 --> 00:10:07,620
Wow. Now who doesn't listen?

212
00:10:10,310 --> 00:10:11,810
Sheldon, your food's getting cold.

213
00:10:11,810 --> 00:10:12,890
What are you doing?

214
00:10:12,990 --> 00:10:17,070
Oh, looking through my textbooks for a new field of inquiry.

215
00:10:17,570 --> 00:10:20,210
Why do we have a Geology book?

216
00:10:20,980 --> 00:10:22,040
Leonard,

217
00:10:22,040 --> 00:10:25,200
did you throw a children's party while I was in Texas?

218
00:10:26,160 --> 00:10:28,070
Wait. What's wrong with geology?

219
00:10:28,070 --> 00:10:30,320
Let me put this in a way you'll understand, Penny.

220
00:10:30,320 --> 00:10:32,130
You remember how you explained to me

221
00:10:32,130 --> 00:10:34,870
that the Kardashians aren't real celebrities?

222
00:10:34,890 --> 00:10:38,950
Well, Geology is the Kardashians of science.

223
00:10:40,590 --> 00:10:43,380
Have you considered studying standard model physics?

224
00:10:43,380 --> 00:10:45,450
You want me to give up string theory

225
00:10:45,450 --> 00:10:47,490
for something that's less advanced?

226
00:10:47,510 --> 00:10:48,840
You know, why don't you break up with Penny

227
00:10:48,840 --> 00:10:51,030
and start dating a brown bear?

228
00:10:52,640 --> 00:10:54,850
Like you could get a brown bear.

229
00:10:55,880 --> 00:10:57,290
Hey, I've got a good job.

230
00:10:57,290 --> 00:10:59,370
I could buy salmon. You don't know.

231
00:11:00,940 --> 00:11:02,550
Maybe you could make your new field of study

232
00:11:02,550 --> 00:11:04,650
the calculation of nuclear matrix elements.

233
00:11:04,650 --> 00:11:05,920
Oh, please.

234
00:11:05,920 --> 00:11:09,760
If I wanted to take up a fad, I'd get a tramp stamp.

235
00:11:12,910 --> 00:11:14,920
What about loop quantum gravity?

236
00:11:14,920 --> 00:11:16,790
Oh, Duchess, look at me.

237
00:11:16,790 --> 00:11:19,760
My quantum gravity's positively loopy.

238
00:11:21,940 --> 00:11:23,260
Who's the Duchess?

239
00:11:23,260 --> 00:11:25,330
One of the people that lives in his head.

240
00:11:26,870 --> 00:11:29,410
I hope he's this distraught if he ever breaks up with me.

241
00:11:29,410 --> 00:11:32,570
Well, if he does, I'll see if my bear has a friend.

242
00:11:34,720 --> 00:11:35,830
Sheldon, have you ever considered

243
00:11:35,830 --> 00:11:37,300
not rushing into something new?

244
00:11:37,300 --> 00:11:38,730
I mean, why don't you take your time,

245
00:11:38,730 --> 00:11:39,930
enjoy your freedom?

246
00:11:39,940 --> 00:11:42,690
Maybe something new to study will find you.

247
00:11:44,250 --> 00:11:46,320
That's actually not bad advice.

248
00:11:46,320 --> 00:11:48,860
You know, I didn't seek out string theory.

249
00:11:48,920 --> 00:11:51,260
It just hit me over the head one day.

250
00:11:51,690 --> 00:11:52,990
How does that happen?

251
00:11:53,080 --> 00:11:55,460
A bully chased me through the school library

252
00:11:55,460 --> 00:11:58,350
and hit me over the head with the biggest book he could find.

253
00:11:59,550 --> 00:12:02,360
Okay, how about we toast your newfound freedom?

254
00:12:02,690 --> 00:12:05,710
Ah, normally I refrain from alcohol,

255
00:12:05,830 --> 00:12:08,970
but since my cerebral cortex is twiddling its proverbial thumbs,

256
00:12:08,980 --> 00:12:10,400
why not soak it in grape juice

257
00:12:10,400 --> 00:12:12,730
that's been predigested by a fungus?

258
00:12:14,370 --> 00:12:17,050
And you wonder why other children beat you with books.

259
00:12:17,050 --> 00:12:18,210
Cheers!

260
00:12:21,650 --> 00:12:23,050
Emily, what do you do?

261
00:12:23,050 --> 00:12:25,870
I'm a dermatology resident at Huntington Hospital.

262
00:12:25,910 --> 00:12:27,420
Mmm, and you know what that means.

263
00:12:27,440 --> 00:12:30,810
Someone's skin's about to be softer than ever.

264
00:12:32,410 --> 00:12:33,860
This is making me crazy.

265
00:12:33,870 --> 00:12:35,900
I know I know you from somewhere.

266
00:12:35,900 --> 00:12:37,690
Oh, uh, Howard was an astronaut.

267
00:12:37,690 --> 00:12:38,980
Maybe that's where you saw him.

268
00:12:39,790 --> 00:12:41,400
That's amazing.

269
00:12:41,400 --> 00:12:42,530
What was that like?

270
00:12:42,530 --> 00:12:45,530
It was the most incredible experience of my life.

271
00:12:45,530 --> 00:12:48,010
Up there in the space station, you're...

272
00:12:48,010 --> 00:12:49,630
Oh, my God, I remember.

273
00:12:51,200 --> 00:12:53,070
Can I finish my astronaut story?

274
00:12:53,780 --> 00:12:55,040
It was four years ago.

275
00:12:55,040 --> 00:12:57,110
- Please don't say it. - We were set up on a blind date.

276
00:12:57,110 --> 00:12:58,990
- Please don't say it. - You came to my apartment.

277
00:12:58,990 --> 00:13:00,350
You're saying it.

278
00:13:01,320 --> 00:13:02,820
Well, wh-what happened?

279
00:13:02,830 --> 00:13:05,320
- Yeah, what happened? - Okay, I'll say it.

280
00:13:06,070 --> 00:13:09,060
I was on the way to pick her up.

281
00:13:09,060 --> 00:13:11,510
My stomach felt a little funny.

282
00:13:11,510 --> 00:13:15,110
When I got there, I asked if I could use her bathroom.

283
00:13:15,180 --> 00:13:16,610
Please don't say it.

284
00:13:19,490 --> 00:13:21,740
One roll of toilet paper and 20 minutes later,

285
00:13:21,740 --> 00:13:24,030
I was so humiliated,

286
00:13:24,160 --> 00:13:27,310
I snuck out the window and never saw her again.

287
00:13:27,310 --> 00:13:28,770
You know what else I never saw again--

288
00:13:28,770 --> 00:13:31,250
my security deposit.

289
00:13:31,880 --> 00:13:32,900
I'm sorry.

290
00:13:32,900 --> 00:13:36,000
I tried to unclog it, but you didn't have a plunger,

291
00:13:36,000 --> 00:13:37,520
and water was spilling out everywhere.

292
00:13:37,520 --> 00:13:39,320
Right, just water.

293
00:13:41,480 --> 00:13:43,740
Look, I have felt terrible about this for years,

294
00:13:43,740 --> 00:13:45,180
and I'm glad I have the opportunity

295
00:13:45,180 --> 00:13:48,020
to tell you just how sorry I am.

296
00:13:48,480 --> 00:13:49,390
It's fine.

297
00:13:49,840 --> 00:13:51,520
All my friends love this story.

298
00:13:51,520 --> 00:13:53,320
They call you Clogzilla.

299
00:13:55,140 --> 00:13:55,690
Get it?

300
00:13:55,700 --> 00:13:57,130
Because you clogged up her bathroom

301
00:13:57,130 --> 00:13:59,450
like a radioactive monster!

302
00:14:00,370 --> 00:14:02,100
Yeah, yeah, I get it.

303
00:14:02,100 --> 00:14:02,900
Howie.

304
00:14:02,900 --> 00:14:05,580
Well, hey, it's not like cotton candy comes out of you.

305
00:14:08,510 --> 00:14:09,700
Come on, let's get you to bed.

306
00:14:09,700 --> 00:14:11,280
You've had a lot to drink.

307
00:14:11,340 --> 00:14:13,640
- No more than Penny. - That's what I'm saying.

308
00:14:14,380 --> 00:14:15,980
Wait, wait, wait, wait, wait.

309
00:14:17,900 --> 00:14:19,100
Empty room.

310
00:14:19,630 --> 00:14:20,860
Empty room.

311
00:14:21,280 --> 00:14:22,530
Empty room.

312
00:14:23,970 --> 00:14:26,900
If somebody says "Come in," I'm gonna freak out.

313
00:14:32,100 --> 00:14:32,770
So, Emily,

314
00:14:32,770 --> 00:14:35,200
why did you decide to specialize in dermatology?

315
00:14:35,350 --> 00:14:37,170
I like cutting people with knives,

316
00:14:37,180 --> 00:14:40,210
and all the other jobs where you get to do that are illegal.

317
00:14:42,390 --> 00:14:43,890
You're kidding, right?

318
00:14:47,830 --> 00:14:50,410
She's scary, but it's a cute scary.

319
00:14:51,840 --> 00:14:53,180
Can I say just one more thing?

320
00:14:53,180 --> 00:14:55,990
I had some kind of food poisoning that day.

321
00:14:55,990 --> 00:14:58,900
Sure, in retrospect, gas station sushi--

322
00:14:58,900 --> 00:15:00,720
maybe not the best choice.

323
00:15:00,910 --> 00:15:03,610
Nor was climbing out your window and running away.

324
00:15:03,620 --> 00:15:05,960
But I think we can all understand how humiliating that was,

325
00:15:05,960 --> 00:15:07,880
and I'd really appreciate it if we could move on.

326
00:15:07,940 --> 00:15:09,530
Howie, we moved on a long time ago.

327
00:15:09,530 --> 00:15:12,110
- I'm just saying... - We moved on.

328
00:15:13,870 --> 00:15:16,180
Okay. Fine.

329
00:15:19,190 --> 00:15:20,200
How's your soup?

330
00:15:20,200 --> 00:15:21,520
Ah, it's all right.

331
00:15:21,580 --> 00:15:23,940
They could've filled the bowl a little more.

332
00:15:27,980 --> 00:15:28,890
Excuse me.

333
00:15:29,030 --> 00:15:29,870
Where are you going?

334
00:15:29,880 --> 00:15:31,220
I need some fresh air.

335
00:15:31,220 --> 00:15:32,300
Been there.

336
00:15:53,600 --> 00:15:55,100
Oh, no.

337
00:15:56,630 --> 00:15:58,120
What have I done?

338
00:16:22,710 --> 00:16:24,100
How you feeling?

339
00:16:25,380 --> 00:16:26,950
Not so good.

340
00:16:28,580 --> 00:16:30,940
Are you gonna introduce me to your friend?

341
00:16:32,390 --> 00:16:33,960
It's not my friend.

342
00:16:33,990 --> 00:16:35,970
Nothing happened.

343
00:16:36,940 --> 00:16:40,550
I don't know. I heard you reading pretty loud last night.

344
00:16:42,340 --> 00:16:44,280
Oh, dear Lord.

345
00:16:45,050 --> 00:16:46,140
Where's Amy?

346
00:16:46,140 --> 00:16:48,330
After she put you to bed, she went home.

347
00:16:50,050 --> 00:16:52,960
I should call her and apologize.

348
00:16:56,500 --> 00:16:58,510
- Oh, no. - What?

349
00:16:58,540 --> 00:17:01,780
Apparently, I called Stephen Hawking last night.

350
00:17:02,990 --> 00:17:04,890
First new message.

351
00:17:04,890 --> 00:17:07,030
Hawkman!

352
00:17:08,280 --> 00:17:11,350
It's your old buddy Sheldonoscopy!

353
00:17:12,900 --> 00:17:15,280
How come you didn't pick up the phone?!

354
00:17:15,280 --> 00:17:17,810
Oh, right. My bad.

355
00:17:23,300 --> 00:17:25,170
You're awful quiet. Everything okay?

356
00:17:26,270 --> 00:17:27,510
Not really.

357
00:17:27,740 --> 00:17:30,640
I had a bit of an embarrassing evening.

358
00:17:30,640 --> 00:17:33,640
Ugh. Me, too. What happened?

359
00:17:33,640 --> 00:17:35,440
I drank alcohol

360
00:17:35,920 --> 00:17:40,130
and may have left an unfortunate voice mail for Stephen Hawking.

361
00:17:41,510 --> 00:17:42,780
I wouldn't worry about it.

362
00:17:42,780 --> 00:17:45,230
He's got a pretty good sense of humor.

363
00:17:45,760 --> 00:17:47,390
Oh, I hope so.

364
00:17:47,980 --> 00:17:50,190
What happened to you last night?

365
00:17:50,360 --> 00:17:51,860
Oh, well, turns out

366
00:17:51,860 --> 00:17:54,890
I'd already met the girl Raj is seeing

367
00:17:54,890 --> 00:17:58,420
when I did a number on her bathroom.

368
00:18:01,490 --> 00:18:03,460
And that number was two.

369
00:18:06,530 --> 00:18:08,970
Well, you know, I've always been a fan

370
00:18:08,970 --> 00:18:11,840
of a story told by Attar of Nishapur,

371
00:18:11,840 --> 00:18:13,270
about a king

372
00:18:13,400 --> 00:18:17,550
who assembled a group of wise men to create a ring

373
00:18:17,550 --> 00:18:19,870
that would make him happy when he was sad.

374
00:18:19,870 --> 00:18:21,640
And that ring

375
00:18:21,640 --> 00:18:25,930
was inscribed with the phrase, "This too shall pass."

376
00:18:27,110 --> 00:18:29,600
- Hey, Cooper! - Oh, my God.

377
00:18:29,600 --> 00:18:32,990
I heard you drunk-dialed Stephen Hawking last night?

378
00:18:33,880 --> 00:18:35,270
Classic!

379
00:18:35,270 --> 00:18:36,800
Get out of here, Barry.

380
00:18:37,290 --> 00:18:38,540
Whatever you say...

381
00:18:38,740 --> 00:18:40,410
Clogzilla.

382
00:18:44,330 --> 00:18:46,640
Clogzilla. That's pretty funny.

383
00:18:48,300 --> 00:18:50,560
I don't think that's gonna pass.

384
00:18:55,640 --> 00:18:57,080
Next message.

385
00:18:57,080 --> 00:18:58,650
It's me again!

386
00:18:58,920 --> 00:19:01,100
I gave up string theory.

387
00:19:01,310 --> 00:19:03,270
You should give up black holes,

388
00:19:03,270 --> 00:19:05,850
and we can totally solve crimes together!

389
00:19:07,800 --> 00:19:09,190
Next message.

390
00:19:09,190 --> 00:19:11,260
Do you know what's great?!

391
00:19:11,320 --> 00:19:14,220
Geology.

392
00:19:15,540 --> 00:19:17,810
Look at this geode!

393
00:19:17,830 --> 00:19:19,620
That's fun to say!

394
00:19:19,660 --> 00:19:24,220
Gee... ode! Gee... ode!

395
00:19:24,490 --> 00:19:25,900
Next message.

396
00:19:25,900 --> 00:19:28,440
Gee... ode!

397
00:19:28,440 --> 00:19:31,090
Gee... ode!

398
00:19:31,240 --> 00:19:33,230
I kiss girls now.

399
00:19:35,350 --> 00:19:36,510
Next message.

400
00:19:36,520 --> 00:19:38,230
Hey, guess who I am!

401
00:19:38,240 --> 00:19:39,660
Beep bop boop bop!

402
00:19:39,660 --> 00:19:41,610
I'm you! Get it?!

403
00:19:43,650 --> 00:19:45,110
Next message.

404
00:19:45,110 --> 00:19:46,400
Are you mad at me?

405
00:19:48,020 --> 00:19:51,300
Oh, no, you're mad at me.

406
00:19:51,300 --> 00:19:53,270
I'm so sorry!

407
00:19:54,040 --> 00:19:55,250
Beep bop boop bop.

408
00:19:56,940 --> 00:19:58,240
Next message.

409
00:19:58,240 --> 00:20:03,550
Thiospinel sulfide. Thiospinel sulfide.

410
00:20:03,680 --> 00:20:06,750
That's even more fun than "gee... ode."

411
00:20:08,130 --> 00:20:09,900
Hey, did you see The Lego Movie?

412
00:20:13,770 --> 00:20:16,090
What a jackass.

